{
  "title": "Alveoli, surfactant and exchange",
  "sources": [
    "CH34S14",
    "CH34S33",
    "CH34S37N",
    "CH34S40",
    "CH34S42",
    "CH36S19"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Exchange surface",
      "text": "In the pulmonary system, an open alveolus provides a surface for gas exchange."
    },
    {
      "start": 5.34,
      "title": "Cell roles",
      "text": "Type one alveolar cells provide structure. Type two cells secrete surfactant."
    },
    {
      "start": 11.0,
      "title": "Surface tension",
      "text": "Notice the inner air surface during expiration. Surfactant lowers surface tension and helps prevent collapse."
    },
    {
      "start": 17.5,
      "title": "Deficiency and collapse",
      "text": "Without enough surfactant, alveoli can collapse and ventilation falls. This is especially important in premature infants whose lungs have not developed adequate production and secretion."
    },
    {
      "start": 28.22,
      "title": "Diffusion and perfusion",
      "text": "Now follow oxygen across the alveolar and capillary walls. Diffusion depends on a thin membrane, a large surface area, and a partial pressure gradient. Blood flow then carries oxygen toward the tissues. Ventilation, diffusion, and perfusion are related, but they are different steps. A change in one step can disrupt exchange even when the others remain intact."
    }
  ],
